<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Kalsia State</h1><p class="st-meta">1760 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Chhachhrauli</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Sikh princely state on the Punjab–Haryana border with its capital at Chhachhrauli, founded by a sardar of the Karorsinghia misl.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurbakhsh-singh-of-kalsia">Gurbakhsh Singh</a>, a Sandhu Jat of the village of Kalsia near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> and a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karorsinghia-misl">Karorsinghia</a> misl, took territory south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutlej-river">Sutlej</a> around 1760. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodh-singh-of-kalsia">Jodh Singh</a> enlarged the holding, and the family made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhachhrauli">Chhachhrauli</a> its seat.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kalsia entered British protection with the other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cis-sutlej-states">Cis-Sutlej states</a> in 1809. It was a small Sikh-ruled state with a mixed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Muslim population, ruled by a Raja and supervised through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab-states-agency">Punjab States Agency</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravi-sher-singh">Ravi Sher Singh</a> acceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1947, and in 1948 Kalsia was merged into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/patiala-and-east-punjab-states-union">Patiala and East Punjab States Union</a>. Its former lands are in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamunanagar-district">Yamunanagar district</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haryana">Haryana</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kalsia-state.jpg" alt="Kalsia State" width="320" height="336"><figcaption>Image: See description · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Ambala_district_(with_Kalsia_State)_of_Punjab_Province,_British_India,_published_in_%27The_Panjab,_North-West_Frontier_Province_and_Kashmir%27_(1916).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1760 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chhachhrauli</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gurbakhsh Singh, Jodh Singh, Ravi Sher Singh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sikhism; Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Punjabi, Haryanvi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kalsia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1854&amp;focus=kalsia-state" aria-label="Kalsia State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6688 1074 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kalsia State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7008 1330L7364 1601M7008 1330L7364 1602M7008 1330L7003 1288"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M7003 1288h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7008 1330h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7008 1330h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1854&amp;focus=kalsia-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
